In Intro.tsx, make the page behind the cover inert for exactly as long as the cover is armed, and blur anything already focused. Swallowing "Tab" alone is a one-liner but misses the address-bar entry (the browser moves focus into the document without the page seeing a keydown) and does nothing about Enter on an already-focused control, so use `inert`:

```ts
// after `locked.current = true;` (line 91)
const shielded = [...document.body.children].filter((el): el is HTMLElement => el instanceof HTMLElement && el !== layer);
for (const el of shielded) el.inert = true;
(document.activeElement as HTMLElement | null)?.blur?.();

// in release() (lines 109-115), alongside the listener removals
for (const el of shielded) el.inert = false;

// in the unmount cleanup's `if (!done)` block (lines 211-216)
for (const el of shielded) el.inert = false;
```

The layer is a direct body child (rendered inside the SmoothScroll fragment), so this covers the skip link, header, main, footer, MotionToggle and TransitionLayer without touching the cover itself. Only the JS path needs it: the no-JS and boot-failsafe paths never show the cover. If the owner prefers the smaller change, add "Tab" to the swallowed keys at line 32 (and rename the set, since it is no longer only scroll keys), accepting the address-bar gap.

In src/components/chrome/Intro.tsx, make everything behind the cover inert for exactly as long as the cover is up. After `locked.current = true; lenisRef.current?.stop();` add:

    // Nothing under the cover may take focus while it is up: a Tab (from the page or from the
    // address bar) would land on invisible controls, and Enter on one scrolls the page under the cover.
    const shielded = Array.from(document.body.children).filter(
      (el): el is HTMLElement => el instanceof HTMLElement && el !== layer && !el.inert,
    );
    for (const el of shielded) el.inert = true;

In `release()` add `for (const el of shielded) el.inert = false;` (release runs on every finish path), and add the same line inside the effect cleanup's `if (!done)` branch next to the listener removals. `inert` does not affect layout, so the FLIP's measurement of `header [data-logo]` is unchanged, and the no-JS / reduced-motion / boot-failsafe paths never reach this code because they return early before the cover is armed.

